WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.243
Secure file handling starts by mapping the
lifecycle.

2
00:00:03.323 --> 00:00:07.079
Identify where files come from, where they
are stored, who can access them,

3
00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:08.709
and which code processes them.

4
00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:12.247
A static image, customer invoice,
temporary PDF export, server log,

5
00:00:12.327 --> 00:00:14.652
configuration file, user-uploaded archive,

6
00:00:14.732 --> 00:00:17.534
and generated backup should not be treated
the same way.

7
00:00:17.614 --> 00:00:21.669
Each has a different source, sensitivity,
retention expectation, access rule,

8
00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:22.643
and cleanup path.

9
00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:26.658
Map trust boundaries between users, APIs,
controllers, servlet containers,

10
00:00:26.738 --> 00:00:30.196
Java services, background jobs, object
stores, local filesystems,

11
00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:33.853
databases, scanners, queues, reverse
proxies, and download clients.

12
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.928
The boundary matters because a value that
is harmless as display text can become

13
00:00:38.008 --> 00:00:41.824
dangerous when it influences a filesystem
path, object key, archive entry,

14
00:00:41.904 --> 00:00:43.990
response header, or processing command.

15
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.528
The same string can have different risk
depending on where it is used.

16
00:00:47.608 --> 00:00:50.887
Separate application-owned files from
user-controlled files,

17
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:54.962
generated files, temporary files, logs,
configuration files, public assets,

18
00:00:55.042 --> 00:00:56.175
backups, and secrets.

19
00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:58.163
Storage layout is a security control.

20
00:00:58.243 --> 00:01:00.389
If logs, secrets, uploads, public assets,

21
00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:04.643
and temporary processing outputs all live
near each other with broad permissions,

22
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:06.750
a small bug can become a larger exposure.

23
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:11.122
Clear separation makes code review and
operations easier because each location has a

24
00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:11.978
known purpose.

25
00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:15.635
Define which operations are allowed per
use case: read, write, append,

26
00:01:15.715 --> 00:01:19.292
rename, delete, list, stream, transform,
archive, extract, or serve.

27
00:01:19.372 --> 00:01:22.770
Also define whether the files are public,
private, tenant-scoped,

28
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.414
user-scoped, administrative, or
internal-only.

29
00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:28.714
Security requirements should be tied to
the business workflow,

30
00:01:28.794 --> 00:01:30.642
not only to the Java API being called.

31
00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:34.180
A safe design starts with policy and then
uses Java APIs to enforce it.
